/**
 * @defgroup Foundation_Workflows_Export Export
 * @ingroup Foundation_Workflows_Delivery
 * @brief Build and install a clean, target-specific distribution package.
 *
 * Export configures the target preset once, builds the installable Release
 * artifacts, removes the default `dist/<preset>` destination, and installs one
 * fresh Release package. The library is always included. Executable API examples
 * are opt-in through `--examples-on` in Bash or `-ExamplesOn` in PowerShell.
 *
 * The exact default sequence is:
 *
 * 1. configure or refresh `build/<preset>`;
 * 2. build `FoundationExportArtifacts` for Release, including optional examples;
 * 3. safely remove the repository-owned `dist/<preset>` directory;
 * 4. install Release into that directory.
 *
 * Debug remains available through the build and test workflows but is not part
 * of the distribution package.
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowExportBash Bash
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/export.sh <preset> [--output <path>] \
 *     [--parallel <jobs>] [--fresh] [--keep] [--examples-on] \
 *     [-- <cmake arguments>]
 *
 * ./scripts/export.sh macos_arm64 --parallel 8
 * ./scripts/export.sh psoc5lp_armgcc_cortex_m3_soft
 * ./scripts/export.sh atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5 --fresh
 * ./scripts/export.sh macos_arm64 -- -DFOUNDATION_SCHEDULING=OFF
 * ./scripts/export.sh macos_arm64 --fresh --examples-on
 * @endcode
 *
 * Examples for PowerShell:
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/export.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 -Fresh -Parallel 8
 * ./scripts/export.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 `
 *     -CMakeArguments "-DFOUNDATION_SCHEDULING=OFF"
 * ./scripts/export.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 -Fresh -ExamplesOn
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowExportArguments Arguments and Defaults
 *
 * | Argument | Default | Meaning |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | configuration | Release | Fixed distribution configuration. |
 * | output | `dist/<preset>` | Installation prefix for the package. |
 * | parallel | CMake default | Worker count for each build. |
 * | fresh | off | Clears CMake cache state before the single configure. |
 * | keep | off | Preserves and overlays the destination. |
 * | examples | off | Builds and installs Release API examples. |
 * | CMake arguments | none | Cache overrides applied to the Release build. |
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowExportPowerShell PowerShell
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/export.ps1 <preset> [-Output <path>] `
 *     [-Parallel <jobs>] [-Fresh] [-Keep] [-ExamplesOn] `
 *     [-CMakeArguments <arguments>]
 * @endcode
 *
 * Export has no configuration selector: distribution artifacts are always
 * Release. CMake arguments customize the single configure/build operation.
 *
 * `--examples-on` and `-ExamplesOn` set `FOUNDATION_EXAMPLES=ON` for that
 * configure operation. Without the switch, the preset default remains `OFF`,
 * so regular packages contain only the library, public headers, and CMake
 * package metadata. Exported examples are Release-only; Debug examples can still
 * be built from the source tree when needed for development.
 *
 * A custom output path requires `--keep` or `-Keep`. This prevents the export
 * workflow from recursively deleting an arbitrary caller-supplied directory.
 * In keep mode, installation overlays the destination.
 *
 * @warning Keep mode can retain files from an older feature selection. For a
 * reproducible custom package, choose a new empty output directory or explicitly
 * clean that caller-owned directory before invoking export. The default
 * `dist/<preset>` path is the only destination the script deletes automatically.
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowExportLayout Package Layout
 *
 * @code
 * dist/<preset>/
 * |-- include/                              Public headers
 * |-- bin/examples/                         Optional Release API examples
 * |   |-- Foundation_Math_Ratio             Release example (macOS)
 * |   `-- FoundationExamples.command        Menu launcher (macOS)
 * |-- lib/libFoundation.a                  Release library (Unix-like targets)
 * `-- lib/cmake/Foundation/
 *     |-- FoundationConfig.cmake
 *     |-- FoundationConfigVersion.cmake
 *     |-- FoundationBuildInfo.cmake
 *     |-- FoundationTargets.cmake
 *     `-- FoundationTargets-release.cmake
 * @endcode
 *
 * Windows uses `Foundation.lib` rather than the Unix archive name, appends
 * `.exe` to example executables, and installs the single
 * `FoundationExamples.cmd` menu launcher. Only Release examples are distribution
 * artifacts. Headers are configuration-independent and installed once.
 * `FoundationTargets-release.cmake` maps the imported target to the Release
 * archive.

 * The macOS `.command` and Windows `.cmd` launchers are console menus intended
 * for double-click use. Each discovers the adjacent examples, runs the selected
 * executable, displays its exit code, and returns to the menu. Terminal owns the
 * macOS window after the script
 * exits; select **Terminal > Settings > Profiles > Shell > When the shell exits**
 * to choose whether a successful launcher closes it. Launchers are installed
 * only when examples are enabled; they are not CTest tests or replacements for
 * the executable artifacts.
 *
 * `FoundationBuildInfo.cmake` records the exported version, platform, system,
 * processor, macOS architecture, compiler, C++ standard, package configuration,
 * ARM CPU/ABI/FPU, and AVR MCU/architecture values, whether executable examples
 * were included, plus the enabled top-level modules. Consumers can load the
 * target with:
 *
 * @code{cmake}
 * find_package(Foundation 1.0 CONFIG REQUIRED)
 * target_link_libraries(MyApplication PRIVATE Foundation::Foundation)
 * @endcode
 *
 * The consumer should set `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` to `dist/<preset>` or set
 * `Foundation_DIR` to its `lib/cmake/Foundation` directory. It must use a
 * compatible compiler, target architecture, ABI, runtime, and Release
 * configuration. Debug consumers should build Foundation from source. Do not
 * bypass the imported target by guessing a library filename.
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowExportFeatures Feature Variants
 *
 * Module overrides are valid because export forwards them into the one configure
 * operation:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/export.sh macos_arm64 --fresh -- \
 *     -DFOUNDATION_FULL_BUILD=OFF \
 *     -DFOUNDATION_SCHEDULING=OFF \
 *     -DFOUNDATION_CONTAINERS=OFF
 * @endcode
 *
 * The generated `FoundationBuildInfo.cmake` records the enabled top-level
 * modules. A consumer must compile with the exported target's propagated macros
 * and headers. Do not merge packages produced with incompatible feature sets.
 *
 * Examples remain demonstrations of the public API, not tests. Export does not
 * register them with CTest or interpret their execution as test evidence.
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowExportCompatibility Compatibility Rule
 *
 * Precompiled libraries are not universal. Produce a separate export for every
 * operating system, compiler/toolchain, architecture, ABI, and incompatible
 * feature set. Every exported package is Release-only. “Any platform” means any
 * target represented by a valid CMake preset and, when cross-compiling, an
 * appropriate toolchain file.
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowExportVerify Verification
 *
 * Before publishing, verify file presence, inspect object architecture with the
 * target tool (`lipo`, `dumpbin`, `llvm-readobj`, `arm-none-eabi-objdump`, or
 * `avr-objdump`), inspect `FoundationBuildInfo.cmake`, and compile an external
 * `find_package` consumer. See @ref Foundation_Workflows_Validation for the
 * complete target matrix.
 */
